Notes for whoever packages this tree, written from the build on the author's bench
(Ubuntu 26.04 LTS "resolute", debhelper 13.31ubuntu1, dh-python 7.20260309,
python3-all 3.14.3-0ubuntu2, python3-setuptools 78.1.1-0.1build1, lintian
2.129.0ubuntu2.1, devscripts 2.26.7 - versions as reported by dpkg -l on 2026-11-07).

Format: 3.0 (native), and why
--------------------------------
This repository *is* the upstream: the protocol notes, the render code, the tools
and the vendor files kept as evidence all live in one tree, developed together, and
`debian/` sits in it as another part of that same work.  There is no separate
upstream release to diff an upstream tarball against, so a native source format is
the honest one; that also means no debian/watch file (there is no upstream tarball
url to point at) and no dfsg mechanism - which matters, because the vendor's files
are the part someone might want filtered out (next section), and with a native
format that decision has to be made by building the second binary package or not,
rather than by a repack rule.  If this is ever uploaded to a Debian-family archive,
expect to move it to 3.0 (quilt) with a repacked tarball, and expect the FTP masters
to ask about themes/alpha/ - debian/copyright answers the question rather than
leaving it to be found.

Version: one number, written in four places - debian/changelog, pyproject.toml,
`xigmalcd/__init__.py`'s __version__, and the wheel's METADATA that pyproject
generates - plus the tag, which is the same number with a `v` in front.  The build
job's "the version, where it is written" step compares changelog with pyproject and,
on a tag, with the tag; tools/selftest.sh compares pyproject with
`xigmalcd.__version__`.  Keep them equal and say so in the changelog when one moves.

Which bytes go in which package
-------------------------------
`xigmatek-connect-lcd` is the code plus what the code needs to look like the vendor
app out of the box: the two Python packages, the three console scripts (`xigmalcd`,
`xigmaui`, `xigmalcd-reset`), the three background clips this project vendors
(4.8 MB), the udev rule, the user unit, the menu entry and the icon.

The udev rule is installed by debian/rules into /usr/lib/udev/rules.d and nothing
reloads udev afterwards - by design, since a rule change belongs to a replug or to an
administrator's `udevadm control --reload-rules`.  `dh_installudev` would not do it
either: it is in the default sequence, no-ops without a debian/<package>.udev file,
prefixes the installed name with its --priority (60 by default, so a file already
called 99-… would land as 60-99-…), and writes no maintainer-script fragment -
`grep -l udevadm /usr/share/debhelper/autoscripts/` on this box answers nothing.
Measured after the install recorded in docs/packaging/smoke-2026-11-07.log: the rule
file present, `/dev/xigmatek-lcd` absent, and no ID_MM_DEVICE_IGNORE in
`udevadm info /dev/ttyACM0` until the device is replugged.  README and docs/packaging.md
say the same in the user's words.

`xigmatek-connect-lcd-themes` is ALPHA's own library - the 27 decrypted layouts,
the clips, the baked overlay PNGs, and the manifest that hashes every one of them
(100 files, 84 926 540 B of vendor bytes).  The 27 `.thm` containers those layouts
were unpacked from are recorded in that manifest by vendor path, size and sha256 and
are not in the package: they were in the 0.1 and 0.2 packages and were dropped at
0.3.  Those bytes are XIGMATEK's and
nobody licensed them for redistribution here; debian/copyright's `OTHER` paragraph
says what was actually done to obtain them and why they are carried.  They are a
separate package so that the act of redistributing them is one deliberate choice at
install time, and so that removing them is one command: `xigmalcd/layout.py`, the
only code that reads the copied layouts, is used by tools/theme_check.py, which no
package installs, and the clips are scanned as one optional root by
xigmaui/clips.py.  Nothing reads them at startup, so the display loop keeps working
with the package absent.

tools/ is not installed by either package.  See debian/rules for the reason, and
docs/troubleshooting.md ("If the panel goes white") for what a packaged system
therefore does and does not offer.

Documentation
-------------
README.md is the overview, and the detail lives in docs/*.md - install.md, usage.md,
gui.md, themes.md, packaging.md, tools.md, limits.md, troubleshooting.md - which the
README links by name.  The whole docs/ set is installed, under
/usr/share/doc/<package>/docs/, so those links resolve on an installed system the same
way they do in a checkout; the transcripts under docs/packaging/ go with them, because
the packaging doc cites them by path.

Why that directory is placed by debian/rules rather than by
debian/xigmatek-connect-lcd.docs: measured with a throwaway package on this box, what a
.docs entry names lands at the root of /usr/share/doc/<package>/ under its own basename
- an entry of `docs/guide.md` installed as `guide.md`, while a *directory* entry keeps
its name.  Since every link in this tree is written `docs/xxx.md` (it has to, for the
checkout and for the GitHub page), the files are installed as a tree so the links keep
working.  README.md and PROTOCOL.md stay at the doc root, as .docs installs them.

The pictures stay out of the package: docs/logo.png, the five docs/preview-*.png,
docs/screenshot-xigmaui.png and the theme contact sheets are 3.5 MB of rendered PNG,
part of it derived from XIGMATEK's theme library, which is the other package's files
and its own licence question.  An installed README therefore shows prose with
repo-relative image links.

Building
--------
    dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b        # -us -uc: unsigned, see below
    lintian ../xigmatek-connect-lcd_*.changes

`--fail-missing` is on (override_dh_missing), so a file pybuild installs that no
.install file names is a build failure rather than something quietly dropped from
the package.

Signing: `-us -uc` means no .deb.signing/debsign signature on the .changes or the
.upload, and no Maintainers signature on the .buildinfo's signature block.  A GitHub
Release attachment carries no OpenPGP signature at all: authenticity there is HTTPS
plus the checksums the release body lists.  That is a real difference from an
archive upload and docs/packaging.md says so where the .deb is offered.

Reproducibility
---------------
Two `dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b` runs of one tree, with SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH pinned to a
moment in the past, come out byte-identical - measured here, both packages, `cmp` clean.
Nothing in debian/rules sets that variable: dpkg-buildpackage does, from the top
debian/changelog entry's timestamp, and debhelper computes the same thing in Dh_Lib.pm.
What bites is that dpkg-deb only pulls archive-member mtimes *down* to that value: if
the changelog entry is dated ahead of the build, every member keeps the build's own wall
clock and two machines cannot agree on the bytes.  That is exactly how the published
v0.1 asset and a build of the same tree on this bench came out 18 B apart with all 37
file md5s and every control field identical - the 0.1 entry was stamped 04:40:00 +0000
while the runner built at 04:12Z.  The entry now carries the time it was actually
written, and tools/selftest.sh fails if the top entry is ever dated in the future again.
zstd's threading is not a variable here: -19 with -T1 and with -T24 on the same 5.6 MB
tar gave byte-identical output.  See docs/packaging.md for the whole write-up.

Testing
-------
    sudo apt install ./../xigmatek-connect-lcd_*_all.deb
    debian/tests/smoke

debian/tests/smoke runs the checks that only make sense after an install: both
console scripts answering --help, the clip that pyproject.toml does not describe
being found under /usr/share/xigmalcd/media by the same root rules a checkout uses,
one frame rendered with no panel in the loop, the menu entry passing
desktop-file-validate, the udev rule and unit present with the unit holding no
--allow-reset, and (if the themes package is installed) every file in it hashing to
what manifest.json recorded.  It works under autopkgtest (`Tests: smoke` in
debian/tests/control, `Restrictions: superficial`) and by hand as above.

That pair has been run, on the author's bench, on 2026-11-07: both packages from one
`apt install` command line, `Setting up xigmatek-connect-lcd` creating
`/etc/systemd/user/default.target.wants/xigmalcd.service` (enabled, not started - the
port was still free afterwards, `fuser /dev/ttyACM0` empty), and then
`all installed checks passed`.  The transcript is tracked at
`docs/packaging/smoke-2026-11-07.log`, including the sha256 of the two .deb files it
installed; docs/packaging.md quotes that transcript and points there.  The same pair has
also run green in CI, on a runner that installed both packages and re-hashed 152/152
manifest entries there.

The offline suite, tools/selftest.sh, runs before packaging in CI and is the thing
to run after editing protocol.py, render.py or xigmaui/.  It is not run from
debian/rules' override_dh_auto_test: two of its sections read the author's bench
files under data/ (the Windows captures, the glass logs) and several measure wall
clock timings, so a build that failed because a runner was slow would be a build
failure that means nothing.

What pybuild does instead, left alone, is worse than nothing.  With no test suite to
find it still runs `python3 -m unittest discover -v` in the built tree, and that
command's result belongs to the machine rather than to the package: here it reports
"Ran 0 tests / NO TESTS RAN", while with the distro's dist-packages off the path the
same command errors out at `from . import protocol, render, sensors` because
python3-serial and python3-pil are runtime Depends, not Build-Depends.  That is what
the first CI build did - `unittest.loader._FailedTest`, `ModuleNotFoundError: No
module named 'PIL'`, `dh_auto_test ... exit code 13` - and the reason it never showed
up on this bench is that this bench has Pillow installed.  debian/rules therefore
exports PYBUILD_DISABLE=test; the suite that actually tests something is the CI job
named above, and debian/tests/smoke is what tests the install.
